{"kind":"assert","text":"TWO DEFECTS IN THE TIER-1 DEPLOYMENT NOTE, found by research rather than by running anything, and cheaper to repair on paper than to discover in a live run. Plus a measured result about what already works. Recorded on the note's own artifact so it flares if the note moves.\n\nDEFECT ONE: THE NOTE CONTRADICTS ITSELF ABOUT THE MIRROR.\n\nAt line 84 the note retires an overclaim from the original design in its own voice: \"GitHub rulesets and branch protections govern branches and tags; an arbitrary ref namespace like refs/seq/* is writable by anyone holding repo write access, and the same is true or configuration-dependent on other mainstream forges.\"\n\nAt line 2192, case 6 then specifies the recovery replica as a \"refs/seq-mirror/\u003cgenesis\u003e replica, ruleset-protected against force-update and writable only by the drain's principal, fast-forward-only.\"\n\nThose cannot both hold. refs/seq-mirror/* is an arbitrary ref namespace, so by the note's own line-84 finding no ruleset reaches it. Case 6 exists because the earlier draft's availability claim rested on a recovery step that did not exist; as written now it rests on a protection GitHub cannot provide. I confirmed the underlying fact independently against GitHub's ruleset documentation: rulesets target branches or tags, and nothing else.\n\nThe repair is one the note already knows. Line 117 records carrying the sequence itself under refs/heads/ as an option for exactly this reason. The mirror should be refs/heads/seq-mirror/\u003cgenesis\u003e, where a ruleset can actually reach it.\n\nDEFECT TWO: A DEPLOY KEY CANNOT BE THE DRAIN PRINCIPAL IF ANY OF ITS REFS ARE RULESET-PROTECTED.\n\nThe note wants refs/heads/claimed/* at line 776 and refs/heads/gitseq/batches/* at line 2251 under rulesets that permit only the drain's principal. GitHub's eligible ruleset bypass actors are repository admins, organization and enterprise owners, the maintain and write roles, teams, GitHub Apps, and Dependabot. Deploy keys are not on that list. So if the drain authenticates with a deploy key, the very ruleset meant to reserve those namespaces for it locks it out.\n\nThis bears directly on case 3, which asks whether GITHUB_TOKEN can push refs/seq/* and says the deploy-key fallback should be the spike's documented finding rather than a user's discovery. The research says the fallback is probably not needed for refs/seq/* — GitHub documents rejection only for its own hidden namespaces, refs/pull/* being the documented read-only one, and pushing custom namespaces such as refs/notes/* is ordinary supported behaviour; GITHUB_TOKEN with contents: write carries no ref-namespace restriction. But wherever ruleset protection IS wanted, the principal must be a GitHub App installation. That is a change to the tier-1 credential design, and it is not what the deploy-key fallback sentence currently implies.\n\nOne further property, recorded because it cuts the other way and someone will raise it: a deploy-key push triggers workflows, where a GITHUB_TOKEN-authored push does not create workflow runs at all. Since the drain is schedule-only by design at line 169, that property buys nothing here. GITHUB_TOKEN is the better choice for refs/seq/*, and a GitHub App is the right choice anywhere a ruleset is wanted.\n\nWHAT ALREADY WORKS, MEASURED. The cheap half of the drain runs today over the existing admission library, on a throwaway log created with gs init in a temporary directory — never against this repository's own log and never over a network. Eleven assertions passed. Case 1, race: two concurrent submitters produced two distinct inbox refs and both were admitted in one linear chain, depth 6 to 8, inbox emptied. Case 1's dedup half: replaying the identical candidate returned replay true at the same commit id with depth unchanged. Case 2a, backlog drain: eight accumulated branches were all admitted exactly once in one pass, in lexicographic order, depth advancing by exactly eight. Case 4's core: four malformed candidates produced zero sequence appends and four distinct reasons from the real library — payload exceeds genesis ceiling, candidate carries a parent, a wrong-ref detection from recomputing the canonical ref out of the candidate's own intent, and not a gitseq event envelope.\n\nThe admission library really is ready to be an intake, which is the one claim in the retired request's conditions that held. app.Workspace.AcceptSubmission at internal/app/app.go:991 is the door, adding the static-actor allowlist and a resident queue depth of 32 over kernel.Submit; intent.Envelope and intent.ParseEnvelope already encode and decode the candidate.\n\nONE DELIBERATE NEGATIVE RESULT, AND IT IS THE USEFUL ONE. After expiring the reflog and pruning, the refused candidate object was gone: git cat-file -e failed. With the inbox ref deleted and no ledger branch parenting the candidate, the refusal evidence dies. That is precisely the failure the note's append-only ledger branch at line 397 exists to prevent, and it shows the ledger is load-bearing rather than decorative. Case 4's retention half cannot be faked with markers, and no marker scheme should be proposed that does not parent the candidate.\n\nAlso worth knowing, and it constrains any schedule-only design: in a public repository, scheduled workflows are automatically disabled when no repository activity has occurred in sixty days. The note states this at line 1861 and it checks out.\n\nWHY I AM NOT FILING A REQUEST FOR THIS. The note repair is small and belongs to whoever next owns the deployment lane; filing it as an unclaimed row would add to a backlog this log already fails to drain. If someone takes it, the scope is: correct case 6's mirror to a refs/heads/ namespace, correct the deploy-key fallback sentence in case 3 to name a GitHub App where ruleset protection is wanted, and record the case-3 research finding so nobody spends a live-GitHub authorization confirming an expected answer.\n\nThe prototype is throwaway and I am not offering it for review. It sits on branch request/sequencer-action-spike at commit 2dadfe14 with its harness and captured results, kept only so the measurements can be re-run. Delete the branch and its worktree when they stop being useful."}